<main class="main"><div class="container"><section class="hero"><div class="breadcrumb"><a href="../index.html">Courses</a><span class="sep">›</span><a href="index.html">Architect</a><span class="sep">›</span><span>Module 6</span><span class="sep">›</span><span>LLMOps Overview</span></div><div style="display:flex;align-items:center;gap:0.8rem;margin-bottom:1.2rem;flex-wrap:wrap"><span class="hero-eyebrow">Module 6 · Lesson 1</span><span class="difficulty-badge advanced">Advanced</span></div><h1>LLMOps & <span class="accent">Prompt Management</span></h1><p class="hero-sub">Master specialized operations for LLMs at scale. Learn prompt versioning, cost tracking, quality monitoring, and safety guardrails. Build production LLM systems serving millions with enterprise governance and reliability.</p><div class="hero-meta"><div class="hero-meta-item"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="10"/><path d="M12 6v6l4 2"/></svg>~42 minutes</div><div class="hero-meta-item"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M2 3h6a4 4 0 014 4v14a3 3 0 00-3-3H2z"/><path d="M22 3h-6a4 4 0 00-4 4v14a3 3 0 013-3h7z"/></svg>5 sections</div><div class="hero-meta-item"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M14.7 6.3a1 1 0 000 1.4l1.6 1.6a1 1 0 001.4 0l3.77-3.77a6 6 0 01-7.94 7.94l-6.91 6.91a2.12 2.12 0 01-3-3l6.91-6.91a6 6 0 017.94-7.94l-3.76 3.76z"/></svg>4 exercises</div></div></section><div class="objectives reveal"><h4>🎯 Learning Objectives</h4><ul><li>Understand the unique challenges of LLM operations versus traditional ML</li><li>Implement systematic prompt versioning and management workflows</li><li>Build cost tracking infrastructure for token-based billing models</li><li>Design quality monitoring for non-deterministic LLM outputs</li><li>Establish safety guardrails and content filtering policies</li><li>Create incident runbooks for common LLM failure scenarios</li><li>Architect end-to-end LLMOps pipelines for continuous improvement</li></ul></div><div class="prerequisites reveal"><h4>📋 Prerequisites</h4><ul><li>Complete mastery of ML operations from Modules 4-5</li><li>Hands-on experience with LLM APIs (OpenAI, Anthropic, Hugging Face)</li><li>Solid understanding of prompt engineering principles and techniques</li><li>Familiarity with cost tracking and billing system architecture</li></ul></div><section class="section reveal" id="llm-challenges"><span class="section-label">Context</span><h2>The Unique Challenges of LLM Operations</h2><p>LLM operations differ fundamentally from traditional machine learning. You cannot retrain models—they're managed as API services. Your "model" is actually code: the prompt template. Quality is non-deterministic and difficult to measure. Costs scale with inference volume, not infrastructure spending. Understanding these differences is critical for building effective LLMOps infrastructure.</p><div class="analogy"><h4>💡 Think of It This Way</h4><p>Traditional ML is like manufacturing: design factories, produce standardized widgets, optimize production. LLMs are like hiring consultants: tell them what to do via prompts, they produce custom work, pay per unit of output. You cannot retrain them, but can refine instructions infinitely.</p></div><div class="infographic reveal"><div class="infographic-title">Traditional ML vs LLMOps Comparison Matrix</div><svg viewBox="0 0 800 300" style="width:100%;max-width:800px;margin:0 auto;display:block"><rect x="20" y="20" width="360" height="260" rx="8" fill="rgba(54,207,201,0.04)" stroke="rgba(54,207,201,0.2)"/><text x="200" y="45" text-anchor="middle" fill="#1A3A6B" font-size="12" font-weight="700">Traditional ML Ops</text><text x="40" y="75" fill="#7A756B" font-size="10" font-weight="600">Model Evolution:</text><text x="40" y="92" fill="#7A756B" font-size="9">Retrain weekly/monthly with new data</text><text x="40" y="110" fill="#7A756B" font-size="10" font-weight="600">Cost Structure:</text><text x="40" y="127" fill="#7A756B" font-size="9">Fixed compute infrastructure cost</text><text x="40" y="145" fill="#7A756B" font-size="10" font-weight="600">Quality Metrics:</text><text x="40" y="162" fill="#7A756B" font-size="9">Deterministic accuracy, AUC, F1 score</text><text x="40" y="180" fill="#7A756B" font-size="10" font-weight="600">Iteration Speed:</text><text x="40" y="197" fill="#7A756B" font-size="9">Weeks to deploy new models</text><text x="40" y="215" fill="#7A756B" font-size="10" font-weight="600">Monitoring Focus:</text><text x="40" y="232" fill="#7A756B" font-size="9">Model performance drift detection</text><rect x="420" y="20" width="360" height="260" rx="8" fill="rgba(255,149,0,0.04)" stroke="rgba(255,149,0,0.2)"/><text x="600" y="45" text-anchor="middle" fill="#C0522F" font-size="12" font-weight="700">LLMOps</text><text x="440" y="75" fill="#7A756B" font-size="10" font-weight="600">Prompt Evolution:</text><text x="440" y="92" fill="#7A756B" font-size="9">Iterate prompts daily, no retraining</text><text x="440" y="110" fill="#7A756B" font-size="10" font-weight="600">Cost Structure:</text><text x="440" y="127" fill="#7A756B" font-size="9">Pay per token used, volume-based</text><text x="440" y="145" fill="#7A756B" font-size="10" font-weight="600">Quality Metrics:</text><text x="440" y="162" fill="#7A756B" font-size="9">Non-deterministic, human evaluation</text><text x="440" y="180" fill="#7A756B" font-size="10" font-weight="600">Iteration Speed:</text><text x="440" y="197" fill="#7A756B" font-size="9">Seconds to deploy new prompt</text><text x="440" y="215" fill="#7A756B" font-size="10" font-weight="600">Monitoring Focus:</text><text x="440" y="232" fill="#7A756B" font-size="9">Cost, quality, safety, hallucinations</text></svg></div><h3>Five Core Operational Differences</h3><p><strong>1. No Model Retraining:</strong> Improvement comes from refining prompts, not model updates. The model is frozen; the interface evolves.</p><p><strong>2. Non-Determinism:</strong> Identical inputs produce different outputs each time (unless temperature=0). This breaks traditional ML monitoring and requires human evaluation loops.</p><p><strong>3. Token-Based Economics:</strong> Costs depend on input+output length. A production incident that increases token usage by 10% multiplies costs by 10%.</p><p><strong>4. Speed of Iteration:</strong> Deploy new prompts in seconds, not weeks. The bottleneck shifts from deployment infrastructure to validation methodology.</p><p><strong>5. Safety Criticality:</strong> Language models can generate harmful content. Content filtering and safety guardrails become core operational requirements from day one.</p><div class="key-takeaway"><h4>✦ Key Takeaways</h4><ul><li>LLMOps requires fundamentally different operational thinking than traditional ML</li><li>Prompt versioning and testing replaces model retraining as primary improvement lever</li><li>Cost tracking at token-level granularity is critical for P&amp;L visibility</li><li>Quality monitoring must accommodate non-deterministic outputs with human-in-loop validation</li><li>Safety and content filtering are production requirements, not compliance add-ons</li><li>Velocity in LLMOps means rapid prompt iteration, not infrastructure changes</li></ul></div></section><hr class="section-divider"><section class="section reveal" id="prompt-versioning"><span class="section-label">Architecture</span><h2>Prompt Versioning and Management</h2><p>A prompt is code. It defines exactly how the model behaves. Yet most teams treat prompts casually: no versioning, no testing, no documentation. This is backwards. Your prompt is more critical than infrastructure code because users interact with it directly.</p><p>Systematic prompt versioning means: every prompt has a version number, git history, test cases, and metadata about performance. You can roll back instantly. You can compare versions side-by-side. You can measure which version works better and why.</p><div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">YAML (Prompt Manifest)</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div><pre><code><span class="cm"># prompts/summarize-news/v2.3.2.yaml</span>
<span class="va">version</span>: <span class="st">"2.3.2"</span>
<span class="va">name</span>: <span class="st">"summarize-news"</span>
<span class="va">created_at</span>: <span class="st">"2026-03-15T14:32:00Z"</span>
<span class="va">author</span>: <span class="st">"alice@company.com"</span>
<span class="va">status</span>: <span class="st">"production"</span>

<span class="va">template</span>: <span class="op">|</span>
  <span class="st">You are a news summarizer.</span>
  <span class="st">Create a 2-sentence summary.</span>
  <span class="st">Focus: who, what, when. Avoid opinion.</span>
  <span class="st">Article: {article_text}</span>

<span class="va">parameters</span>:
  <span class="va">model</span>: <span class="st">"gpt-4"</span>
  <span class="va">temperature</span>: <span class="nm">0.3</span>
  <span class="va">max_tokens</span>: <span class="nm">150</span>

<span class="va">metrics</span>:
  <span class="va">avg_latency_ms</span>: <span class="nm">340</span>
  <span class="va">human_satisfaction</span>: <span class="nm">0.87</span>
  <span class="va">cost_per_call_usd</span>: <span class="nm">0.015</span></code></pre><div class="code-explain"><p><strong>What is happening:</strong> Each prompt version is stored with template, model parameters, test cases, and observed metrics. This enables tracking performance and rolling back if new versions degrade quality.</p></div><div class="why-matters"><h4>🌍 Why This Matters</h4><p><strong>In Product Teams:</strong> Testing prompts systematically prevents subtle quality regressions. A bad prompt can degrade user experience for weeks before anyone notices.</p><p><strong>In Regulated Industries:</strong> Auditors require evidence of which prompt version was used for each decision. Without versioning, compliance becomes impossible.</p></div><div class="key-takeaway"><h4>✦ Key Takeaways</h4><ul><li>Version every prompt like source code: git history, tags, release notes</li><li>Store prompts in version control, never in notebooks or Slack messages</li><li>Create test cases for each prompt with expected behavior patterns</li><li>Track metrics: latency, cost, human satisfaction for each version</li><li>Implement automated testing: compare new prompts against baseline</li><li>Enable instant rollback: keep previous versions in warm standby</li><li>Document changes: why this version over the previous one</li></ul></div></section><hr class="section-divider"><section class="section reveal" id="cost-tracking"><span class="section-label">Economics</span><h2>Token Economy and Cost Tracking</h2><p>With traditional infrastructure, costs are predictable. With LLM APIs, costs depend on usage patterns and token lengths. A single bug increasing token usage by 50% multiplies costs immediately. Systematic cost tracking and real-time alerting is essential for profitability.</p><p>You need visibility into: cost per request, cost per user, cost per feature, cost per model version. You need alerts when costs spike. You need budgets: "this feature costs maximum 5 cents per user."</p><div class="code-header"><span class="code-lang">Python (Cost Tracking Instrumentation)</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div><pre><code><span class="cm"># Track API costs in production</span>
<span class="kw">async</span> <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">call_llm_tracked</span>(
    prompt: <span class="va">str</span>,
    user_id: <span class="va">str</span>,
    feature: <span class="va">str</span>
) -> <span class="va">str</span>:
    <span class="va">start</span> <span class="op">=</span> time.<span class="fn">time</span>()
    <span class="va">response</span> <span class="op">=</span> <span class="kw">await</span> openai.<span class="fn">ChatCompletion</span>.<span class="fn">acreate</span>(
        model<span class="op">=</span><span class="st">"gpt-4"</span>,
        messages<span class="op">=</span>[{<span class="st">"role"</span>: <span class="st">"user"</span>, 
                       <span class="st">"content"</span>: prompt}]
    )

    <span class="cm"># Calculate cost based on token pricing</span>
    <span class="va">in_tokens</span> <span class="op">=</span> response.<span class="va">usage</span>.<span class="va">prompt_tokens</span>
    <span class="va">out_tokens</span> <span class="op">=</span> response.<span class="va">usage</span>.<span class="va">completion_tokens</span>
    <span class="va">cost</span> <span class="op">=</span> (<span class="va">in_tokens</span> <span class="op">*</span> <span class="nm">0.03</span> <span class="op">+</span>
           <span class="va">out_tokens</span> <span class="op">*</span> <span class="nm">0.06</span>) <span class="op">/</span> <span class="nm">1000</span>

    <span class="kw">await</span> metrics.<span class="fn">log_cost</span>({
        <span class="st">"user_id"</span>: user_id,
        <span class="st">"feature"</span>: feature,
        <span class="st">"cost_usd"</span>: cost,
        <span class="st">"tokens_in"</span>: in_tokens,
        <span class="st">"tokens_out"</span>: out_tokens,
        <span class="st">"latency_ms"</span>: <span class="nm">1000</span> <span class="op">*</span> (time.<span class="fn">time</span>() <span class="op">-</span> start)
    })
    <span class="kw">return</span> response.<span class="va">choices</span>[<span class="nm">0</span>].<span class="va">message</span>.<span class="va">content</span></code></pre><div class="code-explain"><p><strong>What is happening:</strong> We extract token counts from the API response, calculate cost, and log to a central cost tracking system enabling attribution by user and feature.</p></div><div class="key-takeaway"><h4>✦ Key Takeaways</h4><ul><li>Track costs at request granularity with attribution to user, feature, model version</li><li>Implement real-time budget alerts: daily and monthly spending thresholds</li><li>Optimize token usage: shorter prompts, fewer examples, better instructions reduce costs</li><li>Monitor token inflation: alert when average tokens per request increase</li><li>Consider caching: for deterministic queries, cache responses to reduce costs significantly</li><li>Analyze cost per metric: what's the ROI of each feature or model improvement</li></ul></div></section><hr class="section-divider"><section class="section reveal" id="quality-monitoring"><span class="section-label">Operations</span><h2>Quality Monitoring for Non-Deterministic Outputs</h2><p>Traditional ML monitoring tracks deterministic metrics. LLM outputs are creative and non-deterministic. Traditional metrics don't work. Instead, combine automated checks (syntax, safety) with human feedback loops (preference ranking, quality scores).</p><p>Key insight: automate what you can (format, safety), build scalable processes for human evaluation. Humans provide feedback that trains automated checkers, creating a virtuous cycle.</p><div class="infographic reveal"><div class="infographic-title">LLM Quality Monitoring Architecture</div><div class="compare-grid"><div class="compare-card"><h5>Automated Checks</h5><ul><li>Output length validation</li><li>Profanity and toxicity filters</li><li>Hallucination detection (fact checks)</li><li>Code syntax validation</li><li>JSON schema compliance</li></ul></div><div class="compare-card"><h5>Semantic Evaluation</h5><ul><li>Embedding similarity to reference</li><li>BLEU/ROUGE scores vs baseline</li><li>Semantic drift detection</li><li>Consistency across variants</li><li>Bias and fairness analysis</li></ul></div><div class="compare-card"><h5>Human-in-Loop</h5><ul><li>User thumbs up/down feedback</li><li>Crowd-sourced quality ratings</li><li>Expert reviewer annotations</li><li>A/B test winner determination</li><li>Root cause analysis for failures</li></ul></div></div></div><div class="key-takeaway"><h4>✦ Key Takeaways</h4><ul><li>Automate safety and format checks: content filters, syntax validation, length bounds</li><li>Use semantic similarity metrics (embeddings) to detect quality drift early</li><li>Build human feedback loops: users and experts provide training signal</li><li>Track preference: which prompts do users prefer across dimensions</li><li>Create dashboards: compare prompt versions side-by-side with all metrics</li><li>Implement feedback flywheel: human annotations improve automated checkers</li></ul></div></section><hr class="section-divider"><section class="section reveal" id="exercises"><span class="section-label">Practice</span><h2>Hands-On Exercises</h2><div class="exercise"><h4>🔬 Exercise 1: Design Prompt Version Control</h4><p>Create a git repository structure for systematic prompt management. Include: prompt templates with parametric variations, unit test cases with expected output patterns, YAML manifests for each version, and automated comparison tooling.</p></div><div class="exercise"><h4>🔬 Exercise 2: Build Cost Tracking Dashboard</h4><p>Instrument an LLM application to track costs by user, feature, and model version. Create a live dashboard showing daily spend, cost per request, trending, and anomalies. Implement alerts for cost spikes.</p></div><div class="exercise"><h4>🔬 Exercise 3: Implement Quality Monitoring</h4><p>Build a monitoring system that checks LLM outputs for: format compliance, toxicity, hallucinations, and semantic drift from baseline. Implement a human feedback mechanism to continuously improve automated checks.</p></div><div class="exercise"><h4>🔬 Exercise 4: Write LLMOps Runbooks</h4><p>Create operational runbooks for: cost explosion, quality degradation, rate limiting, and hallucination spikes. Include diagnosis steps, immediate response, and resolution procedures. Practice executing them.</p></div></section><div class="next-preview reveal"><h4>📍 Up Next: Lesson 6.2</h4><p><strong>Prompt Engineering at Scale</strong> — Build on these foundations to develop systematic techniques for testing and optimizing prompts. Learn large-scale experiments, measurement frameworks, and continuous improvement processes.</p></div><div class="nav-buttons"><span></span><a href="../learn.html?c=architect&l=module6-lesson2" class="nav-btn next">Next Lesson <svg viewBox="0 0 24 24"><path d="M9 18l6-6-6-6"/></svg></a></div></div></main><footer><div class="container"><div class="footer-logo"><img src="../../assets/logo_only.png" alt="SustainSys" class="footer-logo-img"><div class="footer-logo-text">Sustain<span>Sys</span> AI Academy</div></div><p>&copy; 2026 SustainSys. All rights reserved.</p><p><a href="https://sustainsys.co.uk">sustainsys.co.uk</a></p></div></footer><script src="https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/npm/@supabase/supabase-js@2/dist/umd/supabase.min.js"></script>
